      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "7f2c02b2-3d9e-5e6e-bf66-14e9568a816b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: King Conchobar, Cúchulainn, Queen Medb, Mary Colum\n- Places: Ulster, Connacht, Ireland, Lowland Scotland\n- Works: Táin\n- Languages: Scots, Ulster Scots\n- Topics: Irish mythology, literary criticism\n\nFacts:\n- The Táin tells of the exploits of King Conchobar and his chief warrior Cúchulainn, known as \"The Hound of Ulster,\" and of Queen Medb of Connacht invading Ulster to capture the Brown Bull of Cuailgne.\n- Ulster is a province in the north of the island of Ireland.\n- Scots is a Germanic language variety spoken in Lowland Scotland and in parts of Ulster, where the local dialect is called Ulster Scots.\n- Mary Colum, née Maguire, was born on June 14, 1884, and was an Irish literary critic and author who also co-founded a literary journal.\n- Mary Colum died on October 22, 1957.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "3877e3db-f81b-5aca-a0d2-1d8b184c33bc",
        "text": "Page: Louis le Brocquy Táin illustrations. Sentence: It tells of the exploits of King Conchobar and his chief warrior Cúchulainn (\"The Hound of Ulster\") and of the invasion of Ulster by Queen Medb of Connacht in an attempt to capture the Brown Bull of Cuailgne.\nPage: Ulster. Sentence: Ulster ( ; Irish: \"Ulaidh\" ] or \"Cúige Uladh\" ] , Ulster Scots: \"Ulstèr\" or \"Ulster\") is a province in the north of the island of Ireland.\nPage: Scots language. Sentence: Scots is the Germanic language variety spoken in Lowland Scotland and parts of Ulster (where the local dialect is known as Ulster Scots).\nPage: Mary Colum. Sentence: Mary Colum (née Maguire, June 14, 1884 – October 22, 1957) was an Irish literary critic and author, who also co-founded a literary journal.",
